Part A – System Design

1. System Architecture

Architecture: Client–Server using RESTful APIs
Tech Stack:

Backend: Python (FastAPI recommended for speed & docs)

Frontend: React (or basic HTML/JS/CSS)

Database: PostgreSQL or SQLite

Auth: JWT-based authentication with roles

Deployment: Docker + Gunicorn/Uvicorn (for scaling)


Frontend (React/UI) → API Gateway (FastAPI) → Database (PostgreSQL/SQLite)
                               ↑
                       JWT Authentication

2. Core Entities & Relationships

Entity	Attributes	Relationships

User	id, name, email, password_hash, role (student/teacher)	One-to-many (teacher → assignments)
Assignment	id, title, description, due_date, teacher_id	One-to-many (assignment → submissions)
Submission	id, assignment_id, student_id, content, timestamp	Many-to-one

3. API Endpoints

1. Teacher creates assignment

POST /assignments/
Headers: Authorization: Bearer <JWT>
Body: {
  "title": "Math Homework",
  "description": "Solve Q1-Q10",
  "due_date": "2025-08-01"
}

2. Student submits assignment

POST /assignments/{assignment_id}/submit/
Headers: Authorization: Bearer <JWT>
Body: {
  "content": "My answer is attached",
  "file_url": "optional_file_url.pdf"
}

3. Teacher views submissions

GET /assignments/{assignment_id}/submissions/
Headers: Authorization: Bearer <JWT>

4. Authentication Strategy

JWT Auth with role-based access

Roles: student, teacher

Only teachers can create/view submissions.

Students can only submit.

5. Scalability Suggestions

Switch from SQLite → PostgreSQL → cloud DB (e.g., Amazon RDS)

Add Redis cache for frequently accessed assignments

Use S3 for file uploads

Deploy via Docker, Kubernetes for auto-scaling

Use message queues (e.g., RabbitMQ) for file processing

